WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.269 --> 00:00:07.279
WINDOWS XP:
This wallpaper is by far the most recognizable

00:00:07.279 --> 00:00:09.070
wallpaper in the world.

00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:14.950
In fact, this wallpaper may be the most viewed
and potentially most nostalgic photo of all

00:00:14.950 --> 00:00:15.950
time.

00:00:15.950 --> 00:00:19.970
Whether you’re a casual computer user who
just uses computers to check emails or you’re

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:25.150
a PC enthusiast who loves to build your’re
own water cooled systems, this wallpaper unites

00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:26.200
us all.

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:32.029
And it’s not something that we just recognize
either, it’s a blast to the past, a reminder

00:00:32.029 --> 00:00:34.160
of the revolution that computers are.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:39.250
But, there’s been several revolutionary
launches since then but none of those wallpapers

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:42.690
are anywhere near as recognizable or nostalgic.

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:48.440
For example, this was the wallpaper that Steve
Jobs used in the original iPhone keynote in

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:54.140
2007 but unless you’re an Apple fanboy,
you probably didn’t even know that.

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:59.539
And as for Android, has their ever even been
a wallpaper that was iconic of the OS?

00:00:59.539 --> 00:01:06.280
I would say the answer is no, but what makes
XP even more interesting is that it wasn’t

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:08.650
even the first OS from Microsoft.

00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:13.740
In fact, it didn’t come out till 16 years
after the original version of Windows.

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:22.540
Before Windows XP, there was Windows 1.0,
2.0, OS/2, 3.1, NT 3.X, 95, NT 4.0, 98, 2000,

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:24.250
and ME.

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:30.110
So, Windows XP was actually the 12th iteration
of Windows and there’s been plenty since

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:31.400
then as well.

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:36.330
This includes Windows Vista, 7, 8, 10, and
most recently 11.

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:42.490
But despite all these versions, Windows XP
remains OG.

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:47.680
Coincidentally, the iPhone is at the same
age right now as Windows was when XP came

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:48.680
out.

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:53.530
So, that wallpaper going viral is similar
to an iPhone 15 wallpaper going viral and

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:54.600
being remembered for decades.

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:59.580
Now, I don’t know about you but I don’t
think the iPhone 15 will be remembered in

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:02.480
a few decades much less its bubbly wallpapers.

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:07.020
With that being said, it didn’t seem like
XP would be a gamechanger at launch either.

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:11.930
In fact, the cards were very much stacked
against Microsoft from all fronts.

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:17.519
So, join me as we take a look back at what
made Windows XP beat the odds and basically

00:02:17.519 --> 00:02:21.340
became the symbol of computers?

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:33.980
AN UPHILL BATTLE:
Given the success of XP, you would probably

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:38.510
be inclined to think that XP came out during
the peak of Microsoft.

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:44.090
During a time when everyone was excited about
Microsoft and Windows and computers.

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:47.320
This sort of situation is what usually leads
to such nostalgia.

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:52.650
For example, when Gmail, YouTube, and Chrome
came out, most people were rooting for Google.

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:58.720
Google was the internet company of the people,
but the same could not be said about Microsoft.

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:02.190
In fact, it was actually quite the opposite.

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:06.599
You see, Microsoft was actually caught up
in one of the most influential court cases

00:03:06.599 --> 00:03:10.269
of all time: United States vs Microsoft.

00:03:10.269 --> 00:03:15.170
Essentially, regulators were claiming that
Microsoft was a monopoly which they definitely

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:16.360
were.

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:20.489
Regulators were hoping to split the company
into 2: one that was in charge of producing

00:03:20.489 --> 00:03:24.550
operating systems and one that was in charge
of producing all other pieces of software

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:27.099
like Office and Internet Explorer.

00:03:27.099 --> 00:03:32.730
In fact, the only reason that this motion
didn’t go through is because of a technical

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:34.280
legal violation.

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:39.220
The judge that was overseeing the case had
talked with the press while the case was ongoing

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:43.069
which was a violation of conduct, nullifying
the subsequent ruling.

00:03:43.069 --> 00:03:47.940
So, instead of being broken up, Microsoft
would end up settling with the DOJ on November

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:49.530
2, 2001.

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:54.580
But, while Microsoft had narrowly avoided
a breakup, this only made public sentiment

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:56.980
around Microsoft even worse.

00:03:56.980 --> 00:04:00.980
And this was the context in which Windows
XP was launched.

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:08.030
In fact, XP was launched on October 25, 2001,
so just one week before the settlement during

00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:11.689
which consumer sentiment against Microsoft
was worse than ever.

00:04:11.689 --> 00:04:17.100
Too make things worse, Microsoft was becoming
a lot stricter with their anti piracy policy.

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:22.130
XP was the first version that required Windows
Product Activation which just rubbed people

00:04:22.130 --> 00:04:27.470
the wrong way as it just made Microsoft look
greedy and that was just the tip of the iceberg.

00:04:27.470 --> 00:04:32.169
You know how Apple gets a bunch of slack for
not allowing users to customize their products.

00:04:32.169 --> 00:04:37.610
With the Mac Studio for example, if you swap
out the SSD, the computer will no longer boot.

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:41.770
If you ask Apple, they’ll say that this
is “a security feature” that ensures that

00:04:41.770 --> 00:04:47.220
the data on your SSD is safe, but in reality,
it’s just a way to force people to come

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:50.060
to Apple if they need storage upgrades.

00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:54.500
Coming from Apple though, this is really not
surprising but did you know that Microsoft

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:57.810
was doing the exact same thing over 20 years
ago?

00:04:57.810 --> 00:05:02.350
That’s right, XP is dependent on the specific
motherboard chipset that it’s originally

00:05:02.350 --> 00:05:03.960
installed with to boot.

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:08.160
So, if you swap hard drives, the computer
will no longer boot.

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:13.199
The only logical reason for this was to force
consumers to buy a new Windows license with

00:05:13.199 --> 00:05:14.540
each computer.

00:05:14.540 --> 00:05:18.900
So, basically, people were dealing with a
Google style monopoly with the anti consumer

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:20.900
practices of Apple.

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:23.790
This was the context in which XP was launched.

00:05:23.790 --> 00:05:29.310
But, it wasn’t just the context that wasn’t
optimal, XP itself came with a lot of shortcomings

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:30.810
starting with bloatware.

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:34.910
This was actually the main reason for the
anti trust lawsuit in the first place.

00:05:34.910 --> 00:05:39.449
Microsoft was leveraging their OS monopoly
to push crappy software like Internet Explorer

00:05:39.449 --> 00:05:41.300
and MSN Messenger.

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:46.080
This effectively killed the competition within
the browser space from players like Netscape.

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:51.500
But not only was XP filled with bloatware,
it was also filled major security risks.

00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:55.280
Remember how computer viruses were a serious
concern back in the early 2000s?

00:05:55.280 --> 00:06:01.590
Well, a lot of that is thanks to the security
of XP or I guess lack thereof.

00:06:01.590 --> 00:06:06.180
We can really get into the weeds of this one
but essentially, Microsoft went ahead and

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:11.919
connected XP computers directly to the internet
with their TCP/IP protocol instead of having

00:06:11.919 --> 00:06:14.610
some sort of security layer in between.

00:06:14.610 --> 00:06:19.870
This was despite several expert warnings months
before XP even came out.

00:06:19.870 --> 00:06:21.979
And what was the result of all of this?

00:06:21.979 --> 00:06:24.800
Well, XP became a virus magnet.

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:30.410
So, to sum things up, XP came out from a widely
hated monopoly and was filled with bloatware

00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:32.669
and fundamental security issues.

00:06:32.669 --> 00:06:37.449
You would think that such a combination would
lead to a disgraced OS like Windows 8 but

00:06:37.449 --> 00:06:46.090
somehow XP would go onto be the most nostalgic
OS of all time, so what in the world happened?

00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:59.030
THE XP ADVANTAGE:
I know we’ve been largely trashing on XP

00:06:59.030 --> 00:07:03.750
and Microsoft so far but there’s a lot of
great things to say about XP as well starting

00:07:03.750 --> 00:07:05.440
with it’s UI.

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:11.120
Not only was XP easy to use but it was the
first OS that seemed appealing to use.

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:15.180
I mean, until XP, this is how Windows looked.

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:19.710
The task bar was gray, the windows were gray,
the entire OS was gray.

00:07:19.710 --> 00:07:24.910
This is because the main focus until XP was
functionality, not aesthetics.

00:07:24.910 --> 00:07:29.680
People bought computers to draft documents,
research, learn coding, and send emails, not

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:31.140
to mess around with.

00:07:31.140 --> 00:07:35.580
Moreover, Microsoft had their plates full
with building out functionality itself.

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:41.590
But, by the time we got to XP though, Microsoft
was able to focus on the UI side of things

00:07:41.590 --> 00:07:44.970
as well, making computers inviting to use.

00:07:44.970 --> 00:07:51.780
Now of course, XP is nothing in comparison
to 2023 standards but for 2001 standards,

00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:54.030
this was revolutionary.

00:07:54.030 --> 00:07:56.849
Computers had also made massive strides in
terms of stability.

00:07:56.849 --> 00:08:02.350
Yes, they were still very much prone to viruses
but the OS itself was more stable than ever.

00:08:02.350 --> 00:08:08.659
I could go on and point out a bunch of other
advantages of XP but honestly, , I don’t

00:08:08.659 --> 00:08:12.610
think the success of XP has anything to do
with XP itself.

00:08:12.610 --> 00:08:18.940
Sure, it was decently stable OS with a friendly
UI and nice colors but the real reason that

00:08:18.940 --> 00:08:23.600
XP was so successful was because of a larger
trend.

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:27.080
Take a look at this graph of annual PC shipments
for example.

00:08:27.080 --> 00:08:29.870
You can almost see the different market segments.

00:08:29.870 --> 00:08:34.279
Between 1981 and 1992, you have the geek era.

00:08:34.279 --> 00:08:38.860
During this period, it was only hardcore nerds
and geeks that used computers.

00:08:38.860 --> 00:08:44.000
The type of people who learn coding for fun
and like building PCs in their spare time.

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:49.029
Between 1992 and 2002, you have the early
adopter era.

00:08:49.029 --> 00:08:53.700
These people weren’t necessarily super into
computers per se but they could see that computers

00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:55.410
were the next big thing.

00:08:55.410 --> 00:08:59.980
They saw that you could use computers to communicate,
research, and learn more than ever before

00:08:59.980 --> 00:09:02.150
and they wanted to be ahead of the curve.

00:09:02.150 --> 00:09:06.190
And then you have the 2002 to 2010 era.

00:09:06.190 --> 00:09:11.730
This was when the general public finally bought
into the idea of computers and more importantly

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:12.960
the internet.

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:17.610
They saw that you could ask Google any question
you wanted and learn about any topic.

00:09:17.610 --> 00:09:21.460
They saw that you could talk to your family
across the world through Hotmail, Gmail, and

00:09:21.460 --> 00:09:22.720
eventually Skype.

00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:26.540
They saw that you could keep in touch with
your friends like never before with Friendster,

00:09:26.540 --> 00:09:28.360
MySpace, and Facebook.

00:09:28.360 --> 00:09:33.730
Most importantly though, they too saw that
computers were an essential part of the future.

00:09:33.730 --> 00:09:37.850
And it was really all of these internet based
services that sold that vision to the general

00:09:37.850 --> 00:09:38.970
public.

00:09:38.970 --> 00:09:42.180
It wasn’t just people that were coming to
this realization either.

00:09:42.180 --> 00:09:45.440
It was also schools, companies, governments.

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:49.920
All of sudden, elementary schools started
sporting computer labs, old school companies

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:54.690
started buliding IT departments, and governments
organizations started assembling crappy yet

00:09:54.690 --> 00:09:56.420
functional websites.

00:09:56.420 --> 00:10:01.381
All of this led to computer sales rocketing
from a 150 million per year to 350 million

00:10:01.381 --> 00:10:02.490
per year.

00:10:02.490 --> 00:10:05.250
And what did all of these computers have in
common?

00:10:05.250 --> 00:10:11.279
You guessed it, they shipped with XP but this
insane wave of computer adoption only tells

00:10:11.279 --> 00:10:12.410
half the story.

00:10:12.410 --> 00:10:20.040
You see, XP wouldn’t be nearly as iconic
if it wasn’t for the subsequent fall.

00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:32.850
THE BIGGER PICTURE:
Panning over to the post 2010 era, you’ll

00:10:32.850 --> 00:10:36.209
see that PC shipments started falling like
a rock.

00:10:36.209 --> 00:10:40.840
We would see a surge during the pandemic but
that’s already starting to wear off.

00:10:40.840 --> 00:10:43.210
The reason for this isn’t rocket science.

00:10:43.210 --> 00:10:48.240
It’s because smartphones started taking
off which largely reduced the need for computers.

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:52.940
And this had a very interesting impact on
the role of computers.

00:10:52.940 --> 00:10:57.529
People didn’t just get rid of their computers
or anything but computers largely became relegated

00:10:57.529 --> 00:11:00.029
into being a productivity tool.

00:11:00.029 --> 00:11:05.230
Most people use computers to solely do job
related work or school related work.

00:11:05.230 --> 00:11:09.030
This includes writing emails, attending zoom
meetings, working on excel sheets, making

00:11:09.030 --> 00:11:11.850
presentations, writing code, and so on.

00:11:11.850 --> 00:11:15.769
All forms of entertainment have moved over
to smartphones.

00:11:15.769 --> 00:11:20.120
This includes texting, social media, video
calls, shopping, listening to music, playing

00:11:20.120 --> 00:11:22.650
mobile games, and even watching YouTube.

00:11:22.650 --> 00:11:28.320
In fact, almost 2/3s of all YouTube watch
time comes from mobile devices.

00:11:28.320 --> 00:11:32.120
Likely the only exception to this trend is
PC gaming.

00:11:32.120 --> 00:11:36.500
For everyone else though, computers have mainly
become a productivity tool for the most part.

00:11:36.500 --> 00:11:40.940
This is in stark contrast to the early 2000s
when computers had nothing to do with most

00:11:40.940 --> 00:11:41.940
people’s jobs.

00:11:41.940 --> 00:11:46.220
As such, people tended to view computers as
a personal device for emailing your friends,

00:11:46.220 --> 00:11:49.120
and social media as opposed to a work device.

00:11:49.120 --> 00:11:53.209
So, it was only natural that people had a
greater emotional connection to the defining

00:11:53.209 --> 00:11:59.430
aspects of early 2000s computers which happened
to be Windows XP and this wallpaper.

00:11:59.430 --> 00:12:04.950
As computers become work devices though, a
lot of that connection wore off and that’s

00:12:04.950 --> 00:12:09.060
why people don’t have the same nostalgia
towards Windows 7 or Windows 10.

00:12:09.060 --> 00:12:13.699
As for the people who’s jobs never became
computerized, well, many of these people either

00:12:13.699 --> 00:12:17.680
never upgraded or waited a super long time
before upgrading.

00:12:17.680 --> 00:12:24.270
In fact, when Microsoft discontinued XP in
2014, 3 in 10 computer users were still using

00:12:24.270 --> 00:12:27.680
XP, which translated to a quarter billion
people.

00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:32.529
In fact, nearly 20 years after it’s launch,
millions of people still use XP.

00:12:32.529 --> 00:12:38.000
So for a lot of computer users, XP was simply
the OS that they used for the longest time

00:12:38.000 --> 00:12:39.250
making it a classic.

00:12:39.250 --> 00:12:49.980
At the end of the day, XP was by no means
the perfect OS nor was it launched under the

00:12:49.980 --> 00:12:55.610
perfect circumstances; in fact, the circumstances
for Microsoft were far from perfect.

00:12:55.610 --> 00:13:00.820
The circumstances for the PC industry as a
whole however were better than ever.

00:13:00.820 --> 00:13:05.980
And for a substantial portion of the population,
the glory days of using computers for entertainment

00:13:05.980 --> 00:13:11.750
ended up being the XP days and XP is often
the OS they used for the longest time.

00:13:11.750 --> 00:13:15.040
And that’s why this wallpaper is so nostalgic.

00:13:15.040 --> 00:13:19.280
I guess it’s a good thing that Microsoft
didn’t cheap out and spent over a $100 grand

00:13:19.280 --> 00:13:20.360
getting this photo.

00:13:20.360 --> 00:13:25.070
While Microsoft’s foray into the PC world
played out extremely well, the same cannot

00:13:25.070 --> 00:13:26.750
be said about their smartphone ambitions.

00:13:26.750 --> 00:13:32.050
Check out this video to learn more.

